    objectives: ['Say what the people believed about the stone objects and what Abraham saw.', 'Support one simple idea with a sentence from the chapter.', 'Discover what “looked like” tells us, use common irregular past forms (grew, made, thought, saw, came), and notice the difference between “did not” (a fact) and “could not” (not able).', 'Describe an everyday object with “looks like” and “can / cannot”.'],
    pedagogy: 'Start with meaning: learners listen, then find what the people believed and what Abraham saw, each with a sentence from the text. The grammar grows out of the story sentence: you ask “Were the objects real people?” and learners discover that “looked like” is about shape, not about what a thing is. Then they compare “did not eat” with “could not move” and say which one means “not able” before you confirm it.',
