    grammarFocus: 'Targets: looked like + noun (shape, not identity) · irregular past forms: grow → grew, make → made, think → thought, see → saw, come → came · did not / could not + base verb (a fact vs not able).\nNotice (Activity 1): Board “These objects looked like people or animals.” Ask only: “Were they real people? What were they made of?” Learners answer from the text (stone) and say in their own words what “looked like” means before you confirm it.\nBuild (Activities 2–3): match the base and past forms, then choose the base verb after “did not” and “could not” (did not worship, did not eat, could not move). Ask after each item: “Is this a fact, or is it about ability?”\nLikely errors: *did not ate · *could not moved · *thinked / *seed · “looked like” read as “liked”.\nUse (Activity 4): three or four sentences contrasting belief and observation (People believed … Abraham saw that … They could not …), then one sentence about a real object in the classroom (It looks like …, but it cannot …).',
    pronunciationFocus: 'Chunk the long sentence: “They could not MOVE | from one place to aNOTHer, | and they could not HEAR | or underSTAND their WISHes!”\n-ed endings: named /d/, bowed /d/, believed /d/, loved /d/, looked /t/, talked /t/.\n“could not” is often /kʊdnt/: model it once slowly, once at normal speed.\nWord focus: MES-sen-ger, mes-o-po-TA-mi-a, BAB-y-lon, in-TEL-li-gent, HON-est (silent h).',
    beforeReading: ['Put a small stone and a toy animal on your desk. Ask: “What can this stone do? What can’t it do?” Collect three “It can’t …” ideas on the board as predictions for the chapter.'],
    duringReading: ['First listening, books closed: which of our “It can’t …” ideas are in the chapter?', 'Two columns: “People believed …” / “Abraham saw …”, one sentence from the text in each.', 'Underline every “did not” and “could not”.'],
    afterReading: ['Complete the Quick Challenge (why talking to the stone gods was silly).', 'Language Focus: look at “looked like” (Activity 1), practise past forms and did not / could not (Activities 2–3), then use them (Activity 4).', 'Say one sentence about a classroom object with “It looks like …, but it cannot …”.'],
    lessonPlan: '0–4 Hook: stone and toy animal on the desk, learners say what the stone cannot do, three ideas stay on the board; 4–11 Listen with books closed (which ideas are in the chapter?), then listen and read along; 11–17 T-chart in pairs: “People believed …” / “Abraham saw …”, each with a sentence from the text, quick whole-class check; 17–21 Quick Challenge individually; 21–30 Language Focus: Activity 1 as whole-class discovery (Were they real people?), Activities 2–3 in pairs, learners read their answers aloud; 30–37 Use: Activity 4 orally in pairs, then each learner writes one “It looks like …, but it cannot …” sentence about a classroom object and two volunteers read it; 37–40 Exit ticket.',
    discussionPoints: ['What did the people believe about the stone objects?', 'What did Abraham see with his own eyes?', 'Which sentence shows a fact, and which shows that the objects were not able to do something?'],
