    anticipatedMisconceptions: ['Learners only remember the building and miss the final message.', 'Learners add details about the Ka’ba or Hajj that the text does not give; stay with the chapter’s sentences.'],
    grammarFocus: 'Targets: will + verb for a promise · for sure (certainly) · one sentence that answers When? (During that time) Where? (Mecca) How often? (several times) Why? (to see his family) · After + past event · still (continues today) · One of + plural.\nNotice (Activity 1): Board “Ishmael said, ‘I will help you for sure.’” Ask: “Is Ishmael talking about yesterday or about the future? Is he sure? Which words tell you?”\nBuild (Activities 2–3): match the parts of “During that time, Abraham (pbuh) visited Mecca several times to see his family.” with When? / Where? / How often? / Why?, then complete After / still / One of.\nLikely errors: *I help you for sure (no will) · *After Abraham builds · *people visit still · *One of them were.\nUse (Activity 4): four sentences about a shared class task: Our class has an important job. My friend will help me for sure. We meet several times to … . After we finish, … still … .',
